# Existence of a Fricke automorphism from symmetric irreducible Φ

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.exists_isFrickeAut_of_modularPolynomialData`
(`2d98832d-0598-5412-a134-3723f57edf59`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `ac8bcd9f-0e9e-594e-a05b-0859e59473eb`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Fix $N \geq 1$ (a natural number with a `NeZero` instance) and let `data` be a modular polynomial
packet at level $N$: a polynomial $\Phi \in \mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$ which is monic in $Y$, whose degree in
$Y$ equals $\psi(N) = \sum_{d \mid N,\ d \text{ squarefree}} N/d$, and which satisfies $\Phi(j(q),
j(q^N)) = 0$ in the Laurent series field $\mathbb{Q}((q))$; here $j(q)$ is the element `jq` and
$j(q^N)$ is its image `jqN N` under the substitution $q \mapsto q^N$, and the inner coefficients of
$\Phi$ are evaluated at $j(q)$ by the ring homomorphism `evalAtJ`. Assume two hypotheses on $\Phi$:
evaluation symmetry, i.e. $\Phi$ evaluated with inner variable $x$ and outer variable $y$ agrees
with $\Phi$ evaluated with inner variable $y$ and outer variable $x$, for all $x, y \in
\mathbb{Q}((q))$; and irreducibility of `data.toAdjoin`, the coefficientwise image of $\Phi$ in
$\mathbb{Q}\langle j(q)\rangle[Y]$. The conclusion is that there exists a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra
automorphism $\sigma$ of the intermediate field $\mathbb{Q}(j(q), j(q^N)) \subseteq \mathbb{Q}((q))$
generated by the two elements, such that $\sigma$ sends $j(q)$ to $j(q^N)$ and $j(q^N)$ to $j(q)$.

This is the existence half of the Fricke involution on the function field of $X_0(N)$: granted a
symmetric modular polynomial of the expected degree relating $j(q)$ and $j(q^N)$ and irreducible
over $\mathbb{Q}(j(q))$, the generator-exchanging automorphism exists. It feeds the unconditional
statement [`ModularCurve.exists_isFrickeAut`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_isFrickeAut), from which
the Fricke involution and the Atkin–Lehner theory at level $N$ are built.

